# creator5-fw-builder — build configuration
# Edit paths to match your machine, then run `make build`.

# ---- identity of the package you are building -------------------------------
MOD_NAME=anvil                  # goes into the .tgz filename
MOD_VER=""                    # empty = today's UTC date, e.g. 20260823.
                              # Pin it to rebuild an old release, or for a
                              # second release on the same day (20260823b).

# Version stamped on the software component. Empty = keep the stock number
# (recommended). The installer wipes every other version dir before renaming
# its temp dir, so a collision is not a problem; a suffix like 1.9.7-mod is
# only useful if you want to see at a glance that a machine is modded.
SW_VER=""

# ---- which printer is this for? --------------------------------------------
# Creator5Pro / 0029   or   Creator5 / 0028
# Find it on the printer: Settings -> About, or in
# /usr/data/firmwareRes/config/general.json ("machineName").
#
# This MUST match the model the stock package was built for. A package's
# runFirmwareExe.sh carries its own MACHINE=/PID= gate and refuses to install
# on the other model, so a Pro cannot be recovered or modded with a non-Pro
# package (and vice versa).
TARGET_MACHINE=Creator5Pro

# Override for one command:  MODEL=Creator5 make build
# Build both at once:        make release

# ---- inputs (sources you already have on this machine) ----------------------
# Everything below is relative to this. Point it wherever you keep the
# firmware and the source repos; nothing here is committed (config.env is
# gitignored, this file is the template).
DESKTOP="$HOME/Desktop"

# Stock OTA packages, ONE PER MODEL. They are not interchangeable: each
# carries a different firmwareExe and refuses to install on the other model.
# The one matching TARGET_MACHINE (or MODEL=) is selected automatically.
STOCK_TGZ_CREATOR5PRO="$DESKTOP/Creator5-1.9.7-1.2.9-20260810/Creator5Pro-1.9.7-1.2.9-20260810.tgz"
STOCK_TGZ_CREATOR5="$DESKTOP/Creator5-1.9.7-1.2.9-20260810/Creator5-1.9.7-1.2.9-20260810.tgz"

# Set this only to force one specific package regardless of model.
STOCK_TGZ=""

# KLIPPER_FORK USED TO BE HERE and pointed the build at a local checkout of
# the fork. It is gone: the Klipper build is pkg/klipper now, and a recipe
# names its source exactly once -- a checkout beside the pinned tarball is the
# second source that rule forbids, and the one that let a release ship a tree
# nobody had built. To build your own fork, put its tarball in vendor/ and
# re-pin KLIPPER_VERSION and KLIPPER_SHA256 in versions.env; the sha is then
# the same guarantee every other source in this repo carries.

# The toolchanger mod (klippy-extras + configs + helixscreen db entry) used to
# be a TOOLCHANGE= path to a creator5-toolchange checkout. It now lives in this
# repo under payload/klipper/, so there is nothing to set. BUILD_TOOLCHANGE=0
# here still turns it off.

# Mainsail, the HelixScreen build for the Creator 5 (MIPS static) and Moonraker
# are NOT set here. They are downloaded on demand into vendor/ by
# bin/fetch-assets.sh, pinned by version and sha256 in versions.env, so the
# repo carries no binaries. Set any of these ONLY to build against your own
# local copy.
# NOTE: the path is still checksummed against versions.env, and on a mismatch
# fetch-assets.sh OVERWRITES it with the pinned release -- SKIP does not save
# you, it re-downloads too. To build against your own file, also put ITS
# sha256 in versions.env; only an exact match is left alone ("cached"):
#HELIX_TGZ="$DESKTOP/helixscreen-creator5-v0.99.115.tar.gz"
#MAINSAIL_ZIP="$DESKTOP/mainsail.zip"
#MOONRAKER_TGZ="$DESKTOP/moonraker-v0.9.3.tar.gz"

# Optional extras -- leave empty to skip that feature
BUSYBOX_BIN="$DESKTOP/creator5/busybox"          # optional richer busybox

# ---- what the installed image does at boot ----------------------------------
# NOTHING, from here. There is no build-time knob for boot behaviour any more,
# and no runtime config file on the printer either: /usr/data/anvil/anvil.conf
# is gone. Every component that is installed runs, and the settings that used
# to live in that file -- the splash, the startup wait and its timeout, the
# first-boot import, the camera resolution, the nice values -- are now stated
# once each, in the service that uses them, under
# pkgs/anvil-core/payload/etc/s6-rc/source/.
#
# A printer that should not run a component does not have it: leave it out of
# the build with the BUILD_* flags, or `opkg remove` it on the printer. What
# there is no longer a way to ask for is a component that is installed and
# switched off -- a half-installed mod nobody chose on purpose, that everything
# downstream had to keep a second answer for.
#
# The BUILD_* flags that decide what goes INTO a package (BUILD_TOOLCHANGE,
# BUILD_MAINSAIL, BUILD_MOONRAKER, BUILD_HELIX) are not set
# here: bin/common.sh defaults them to the full firmware, and setting one in
# this file overrides that. Only touch them if you want a piece left out.

# ---- root login shipped in the image ---------------------------------------
# This is the whole "enable ssh" feature. The stock rootfs already runs
# dropbear from /etc/init.d/S50dropbear, so port 22 is ALREADY open on a
# stock printer -- there is simply no published root password.
#
# Empty = the INSTALLER picks a random password on the printer -- on the
# FIRST install only, updates keep the one the machine already has -- and
# writes it to anvil-password.txt on the USB stick you flashed from. Every
# machine gets a different one, which a hash baked into a shared package
# never could.
# Set one here to choose your own:   make passwd
# (it prompts, and runs inside the build image -- the same python everywhere,
# where a host openssl may lack `passwd -6` entirely)
ROOT_PW_HASH=""

# ---- crypto (from /usr/prog/bin/unTar on the printer) -----------------------
FF_KEY='FFP0331&*%root'

# ---- the printer replica -----------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here. The replica's settings -- the factory image, the partition
# sizes, a prebuilt printer image -- live in test.env, because they exist only
# for the tests and must never reach a printer:
#
#     cp test.env.example test.env
